00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:31.410
The wedding was great.

271
00:10:31.410 --> 00:10:34.480
It was attended by our mutual friend, Ted MacDonald.

272
00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:35.313
Teddy Mac.

273
00:10:35.313 --> 00:10:37.183
Just might be dropping by later today.

274
00:10:38.807 --> 00:10:41.810
More about Ted a little bit later.

275
00:10:41.810 --> 00:10:43.030
But yeah, it was great.

276
00:10:43.030 --> 00:10:45.520
My wife is still there, the boys stayed longer too.

277
00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:47.640
They just got back last night.

278
00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:49.900
It was really a cool experience,

279
00:10:49.900 --> 00:10:51.290
but there was some interesting things

280
00:10:51.290 --> 00:10:52.450
that happened when I was there,

281
00:10:52.450 --> 00:10:54.139
not the least of which was

282
00:10:54.139 --> 00:10:57.680
there was a death on the Via ferrata,

283
00:10:57.680 --> 00:10:58.950
do you know what the Via ferrata is?

284
00:10:58.950 --> 00:11:00.582
I do not.

285
00:11:00.582 --> 00:11:01.415
So Via ferrata is a term,

286
00:11:01.415 --> 00:11:03.050
I don't know the exact translation,

287
00:11:03.050 --> 00:11:07.410
but it's a general term for climbs

288
00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:10.886
where there are cables kind of pinned

289
00:11:10.886 --> 00:11:15.886
into the side of a raw like rock face.

290
00:11:15.930 --> 00:11:17.130
Like half dome has.

291
00:11:17.130 --> 00:11:19.560
Sort of, and it allows you to kind of clip in

292
00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:24.406
and go, go laterally, like horizontally along the rock face.

293
00:11:24.406 --> 00:11:26.850
Oh, on the face.

294
00:11:26.850 --> 00:11:28.900
So you're like thousands of feet up

295
00:11:28.900 --> 00:11:30.303
but you're clipped in

296
00:11:30.303 --> 00:11:33.670
and it's quite a tourist attraction in Telluride,

297
00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:34.980
this particular Via ferrata.

298
00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:37.097
And every time I go, people are like,

299
00:11:37.097 --> 00:11:38.830
"You gotta do the via ferrata, you gotta do it."

300
00:11:38.830 --> 00:11:41.280
It's like I'm being bullied into doing the via ferrata.

301
00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:42.490
I'm terrified of heights.

302
00:11:42.490 --> 00:11:44.330
I'm always like, "I'm good.

303
00:11:44.330 --> 00:11:46.410
I'm not doing the via ferrata.

304
00:11:46.410 --> 00:11:48.160
Stop with the via ferrata.

305
00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:51.103
Just feel like I'll be trapped,

306
00:11:51.103 --> 00:11:53.130
clipped in on this rock face,

307
00:11:53.130 --> 00:11:54.460
looking thousands of feet down,

308
00:11:54.460 --> 00:11:56.560
terrified and unable to like escape

309
00:11:56.560 --> 00:11:57.393
or get out of it.

310
00:11:57.393 --> 00:11:59.465
It sounds like a mafia.
It's probably why

311
00:11:59.465 --> 00:12:00.434
I should do it.

312
00:12:00.434 --> 00:12:02.190
Like in a mafia assassination plot.

313
00:12:02.190 --> 00:12:04.610
Hey, Rich, come with me to the via ferrata,

314
00:12:04.610 --> 00:12:06.173
and you don't need anything,

315
00:12:06.173 --> 00:12:07.296
don't bringing anything.

316
00:12:07.296 --> 00:12:09.755
They say you need to clip in, you don't have to worry.

317
00:12:09.755 --> 00:12:11.300
You don't need anything, trust me.

318
00:12:11.300 --> 00:12:12.480
We laugh and we just,

319
00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:16.240
but there are tours like proper tours

320
00:12:16.240 --> 00:12:17.820
where a guide will take you across

321
00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:19.520
and it's all safe and you're always clipped in

322
00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:20.550
and all of this,

323
00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:22.960
but apparently two women went up on it

324
00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:24.652
while we were in town

325
00:12:24.652 --> 00:12:27.820
without a guide doing it on their own.

326
00:12:27.820 --> 00:12:31.010
And a woman apparently unclipped

327
00:12:31.010 --> 00:12:34.000
for reasons on unbeknownst to anybody

328
00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:35.760
and fell to her death,

329
00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:38.520
I think like a thousand feet.

330
00:12:38.520 --> 00:12:42.471
So she's walking face down on this thing or whatever.

331
00:12:42.471 --> 00:12:43.714
You're just kind of going,

332
00:12:43.714 --> 00:12:45.870
you're like facing the wall

333
00:12:45.870 --> 00:12:49.442
and you're moving laterally on a ledge.

334
00:12:49.442 --> 00:12:51.176
And you have two clips.

335
00:12:51.176 --> 00:12:53.866
So you unclip one to move

336
00:12:53.866 --> 00:12:56.840
and you always have one clipped in.

337
00:12:56.840 --> 00:12:58.430
So I don't know why this person

338
00:12:58.430 --> 00:13:00.900
would have been completely unclipped,

339
00:13:00.900 --> 00:13:02.170
but apparently that's what happened

340
00:13:02.170 --> 00:13:05.094
and she perished.

341
00:13:05.094 --> 00:13:08.500
I had dinner with David Holbrook,

342
00:13:08.500 --> 00:13:11.160
who's the founder of a thing called Original Thinkers,

343
00:13:11.160 --> 00:13:13.570
which has sort of Aspen ideas,

344
00:13:13.570 --> 00:13:15.930
Nantucket Project sort of festival

345
00:13:15.930 --> 00:13:17.590
that I've spoken at at the past,

346
00:13:17.590 --> 00:13:19.370
it's a really cool event.

347
00:13:19.370 --> 00:13:20.203
In Telluride?

348
00:13:20.203 --> 00:13:21.420
In Telluride, yeah.

349
00:13:21.420 --> 00:13:23.590
He lives there, he's a filmmaker.

350
00:13:23.590 --> 00:13:27.910
The son interestingly of Richard Holbrooke.

351
00:13:27.910 --> 00:13:29.690
And he's a filmmaker, he made a documentary

352
00:13:29.690 --> 00:13:31.090
about his father called "The Diplomat"

353
00:13:31.090 --> 00:13:32.800
that you can run on HBO.

354
00:13:32.800 --> 00:13:37.070
Super interesting guy who was living in New York city

355
00:13:37.070 --> 00:13:41.630
and relocated his family to Telluride in like 2013.

356
00:13:41.630 --> 00:13:43.023
And his son who's 19,

357
00:13:44.397 --> 00:13:47.650
he's one of the people that takes groups up

358
00:13:47.650 --> 00:13:48.880
on the via ferrata,

359
00:13:48.880 --> 00:13:51.660
he's like a really accomplished climber,

360
00:13:51.660 --> 00:13:56.070
but he also participates in search and rescue.

361
00:13:56.070 --> 00:13:58.290
So he was part of the search and rescue team

362
00:13:58.290 --> 00:14:01.430
that had to recover this woman's body.

363
00:14:01.430 --> 00:14:04.200
And we saw him, he was quite shaken up by that.

364
00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:07.860
So yeah, man, that's stuff's for real.

365
00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:10.710
But other than that, Telluride is just,

366
00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:12.870
it's unbelievable, had a great time.

367
00:14:12.870 --> 00:14:16.321
And I think what I wanted to share about that experience

368
00:14:16.321 --> 00:14:18.080
is getting out of LA

369
00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:20.140
and kind of putting some distance between me

370
00:14:20.140 --> 00:14:22.710
and my work, et cetera.

371
00:14:22.710 --> 00:14:24.500
It's one of those experiences that allows you

372
00:14:24.500 --> 00:14:28.050
to kind of reflect on what you're doing with your life,

373
00:14:28.050 --> 00:14:30.840
how you're doing it, things that you wanna change.

374
00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:32.570
I did a bit of a life review

375
00:14:32.570 --> 00:14:34.690
and had a bit of epiphany

376
00:14:34.690 --> 00:14:38.789
around the systems that we have here at the podcast

377
00:14:38.789 --> 00:14:42.410
and what we can change, what we should change,

378
00:14:42.410 --> 00:14:45.000
what we should hold on to, what we should let go of.

379
00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:48.709
And it really connected me to this profound

380
00:14:48.709 --> 00:14:53.480
kind of tendency toward perfectionism that I have

381
00:14:53.480 --> 00:14:56.184
and balancing that against efficiency,

382
00:14:56.184 --> 00:14:59.810
because I think I have this proclivity

383
00:14:59.810 --> 00:15:01.691
towards being a perfectionist,

384
00:15:01.691 --> 00:15:04.710
this idea that I'm the only one who can do this

385
00:15:04.710 --> 00:15:05.543
and I have to do it

386
00:15:05.543 --> 00:15:07.210
and it has to be this particular way,

387
00:15:07.210 --> 00:15:09.220
and if it's not, it's a failure.

388
00:15:09.220 --> 00:15:13.320
And I think I've held onto that because,

389
00:15:13.320 --> 00:15:15.320
or I've created a narrative around that,

390
00:15:15.320 --> 00:15:19.346
that that has fueled part of my success,

391
00:15:19.346 --> 00:15:24.346
but at the behest of kind of hamstringing us

392
00:15:25.120 --> 00:15:28.950
in terms of how we grow and how we kind of expand

393
00:15:28.950 --> 00:15:33.080
and the many ways in which

394
00:15:33.080 --> 00:15:37.320
I think it continues to hold me back and the show back

395
00:15:37.320 --> 00:15:39.440
and reflecting on how I could get

396
00:15:39.440 --> 00:15:41.120
into a head space of letting go

397
00:15:41.120 --> 00:15:42.410
of some of those things

398
00:15:42.410 --> 00:15:45.350
so that we can work a little bit more loosely

399
00:15:45.350 --> 00:15:49.000
and more efficiently with how we do things,

400
00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:51.840
'cause I think there's positives

401
00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:53.710
and pitfalls of perfectionism

402
00:15:53.710 --> 00:15:55.073
based on my experience.

403
00:15:56.070 --> 00:15:59.810
For me, it's allowed me to set a very high standard

404
00:15:59.810 --> 00:16:01.670
and strive to adhere to that.

405
00:16:01.670 --> 00:16:04.550
But ultimately I end up becoming the bottleneck

406
00:16:04.550 --> 00:16:05.660
for every decision.

407
00:16:05.660 --> 00:16:08.190
And now we're running this operation

408
00:16:08.190 --> 00:16:10.700
where there's a lot of people involved.

409
00:16:10.700 --> 00:16:12.930
We have a brand new hire, Dan Drake is here,

410
00:16:12.930 --> 00:16:16.113
shout out to Dan, his first day.

411
00:16:16.113 --> 00:16:19.570
And so that involves managing people,

412
00:16:19.570 --> 00:16:21.380
that involves empowering people,

413
00:16:21.380 --> 00:16:23.190
that involves a decision tree

414
00:16:23.190 --> 00:16:25.360
where lots of decisions are getting made.

415
00:16:25.360 --> 00:16:26.760
And we have a system right now

416
00:16:26.760 --> 00:16:29.150
where I ended up having to yay or nay

417
00:16:29.150 --> 00:16:31.760
a lot of decisions that I really shouldn't

418
00:16:31.760 --> 00:16:33.379
be in a position to do.

419
00:16:33.379 --> 00:16:37.760
And I think like it's part and parcel

420
00:16:37.760 --> 00:16:41.070
of what leads to burnout and exhaustion.

421
00:16:41.070 --> 00:16:42.470
Like when I was in Telluride,

422
00:16:42.470 --> 00:16:44.990
I still had to get a show up.

423
00:16:44.990 --> 00:16:46.620
And then the night that I was flying back,

424
00:16:46.620 --> 00:16:47.790
there was a show going up

425
00:16:47.790 --> 00:16:50.160
and I couldn't just take a break

426
00:16:50.160 --> 00:16:52.370
because the post production cycle,

427
00:16:52.370 --> 00:16:54.620
and I think a lot of people don't realize this,

428
00:16:54.620 --> 00:16:56.713
is really protracted with this show.

429
00:16:57.999 --> 00:17:00.840
We get this blog post up

430
00:17:00.840 --> 00:17:02.140
and we have all these photographs

431
00:17:02.140 --> 00:17:02.973
and how do we create,

432
00:17:02.973 --> 00:17:05.380
it's like publishing a magazine for every episode,

433
00:17:05.380 --> 00:17:08.224
because the web piece of the show

434
00:17:08.224 --> 00:17:13.000
is kind of an aesthetic cornerstone to the whole thing.

435
00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:15.570
And yet the truth is,

436
00:17:15.570 --> 00:17:18.520
I don't know if anybody who listens to this show

437
00:17:18.520 --> 00:17:20.920
goes to the blog post and the webpage

438
00:17:20.920 --> 00:17:22.570
to listen to it or to watch it.

439
00:17:22.570 --> 00:17:26.913
It looks great but we're spending so much time

440
00:17:26.913 --> 00:17:28.840
getting that up on its feet

441
00:17:28.840 --> 00:17:33.240
when truly it's not really that related

442
00:17:33.240 --> 00:17:36.040
to moving the needle in terms of.

443
00:17:36.040 --> 00:17:37.080
But you've always had that.

444
00:17:37.080 --> 00:17:39.770
Like when I was a guest on the show years ago,

445
00:17:39.770 --> 00:17:41.350
I was struck by how everything,

446
00:17:41.350 --> 00:17:45.640
like the look is all very cool and stylized and unique,

447
00:17:45.640 --> 00:17:49.940
and that has always been part of your package.

448
00:17:49.940 --> 00:17:51.470
And I think it has set it apart.

449
00:17:51.470 --> 00:17:52.830
It must've attracted visitors,

450
00:17:52.830 --> 00:17:55.090
having that aesthetic has always been part

451
00:17:55.090 --> 00:17:56.577
of your doing.

452
00:17:56.577 --> 00:17:59.190
Sure, and that's what keeps me wedded to it

453
00:17:59.190 --> 00:18:00.580
at the same time.

454
00:18:00.580 --> 00:18:02.670
However, like when I listen to a podcast,

455
00:18:02.670 --> 00:18:03.870
I never go to the website.

456
00:18:03.870 --> 00:18:05.970
I think the percentage of people

457
00:18:05.970 --> 00:18:09.010
that go and visit that page is probably really small.

458
00:18:09.010 --> 00:18:11.859
And when you balance that against the number of hours

459
00:18:11.859 --> 00:18:15.050
and all the toil that goes into creating that,

460
00:18:15.050 --> 00:18:17.690
the question then becomes, is that worth it?

461
00:18:17.690 --> 00:18:19.819
Especially when we're putting out a book every year

462
00:18:19.819 --> 00:18:24.170
where people can have that experience in book form

463
00:18:24.170 --> 00:18:27.350
versus trying to make that happen every single week.

464
00:18:27.350 --> 00:18:30.210
However, in the process of creating those pages,

465
00:18:30.210 --> 00:18:32.710
we're doing a lot of the legwork for social stuff.

466
00:18:33.689 --> 00:18:38.375
But I wanna work towards a place where

467
00:18:38.375 --> 00:18:40.740
as soon as we're done doing the podcast,

468
00:18:40.740 --> 00:18:42.610
I can walk away and I don't have to worry about it,

469
00:18:42.610 --> 00:18:45.420
which is the way most people do it.

470
00:18:45.420 --> 00:18:47.370
Other people get it up on its feet,

471
00:18:47.370 --> 00:18:49.050
you trust them, it's good.

472
00:18:49.050 --> 00:18:50.370
You move onto the next thing.

473
00:18:50.370 --> 00:18:52.150
And if I could free up that time,

474
00:18:52.150 --> 00:18:53.600
would it not be better

475
00:18:53.600 --> 00:18:56.800
to add another show or two every month?

476
00:18:56.800 --> 00:18:59.380
Would that be more impactful or important

477
00:18:59.380 --> 00:19:03.950
than creating this pristine web page for posterity?

478
00:19:03.950 --> 00:19:06.020
I don't know, so that's just one of the things

479
00:19:06.020 --> 00:19:09.170
that has been on my mind

480
00:19:09.170 --> 00:19:11.450
and I'm not sure how to solve that.

481
00:19:11.450 --> 00:19:14.738
And as somebody who's always been kind of a solo operator,

482
00:19:14.738 --> 00:19:17.080
I struggle with growth

483
00:19:17.080 --> 00:19:19.800
and the responsibility that comes with that,

484
00:19:19.800 --> 00:19:24.000
because I think managing people and figuring out systems

485
00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:28.960
really doesn't meet my strengths nor my past experience.

486
00:19:28.960 --> 00:19:31.370
But this is what growth is, right?

487
00:19:31.370 --> 00:19:32.630
So, anyway.

488
00:19:32.630 --> 00:19:34.650
The other thing I will share

489
00:19:34.650 --> 00:19:35.960
and we'll put the Telluride trip to rest

490
00:19:35.960 --> 00:19:40.623
is on the way back, the travel saga continued,

491
00:19:42.030 --> 00:19:46.620
because we made our connecting flight in Phoenix,

492
00:19:46.620 --> 00:19:48.060
but in the Phoenix airport,

493
00:19:48.060 --> 00:19:51.220
there was a guy who was kind of creating

494
00:19:51.220 --> 00:19:52.700
a little bit of drama.

495
00:19:52.700 --> 00:19:54.070
And I knew that if he got on our plane,

496
00:19:54.070 --> 00:19:55.580
there was probably gonna be a problem.

497
00:19:55.580 --> 00:19:57.960
He was sort of flagrantly taking his mask off

498
00:19:57.960 --> 00:20:00.090
and he was hitting his vape pen.

499
00:20:00.090 --> 00:20:02.040
In a lounge.

500
00:20:02.040 --> 00:20:03.982
In the airport terminal.

501
00:20:03.982 --> 00:20:06.750
Because he was trying to attract attention,

502
00:20:06.750 --> 00:20:09.700
like his goal was to be the center of attention.

503
00:20:09.700 --> 00:20:11.100
Not only does he get on our plane,

504
00:20:11.100 --> 00:20:12.700
he ends up sitting right behind.

505
00:20:14.124 --> 00:20:16.960
And the volume just turned up to 11

506
00:20:16.960 --> 00:20:19.440
on this guy trying to attract attention to himself.

507
00:20:19.440 --> 00:20:23.020
And he was going to do it through charm or jokes

508
00:20:23.020 --> 00:20:26.730
or the volume of his voice, or if need be conflict,

509
00:20:26.730 --> 00:20:28.319
all of which ensued

510
00:20:28.319 --> 00:20:31.040
to the point where finally

511
00:20:31.040 --> 00:20:33.740
a gaggle of flight attendants descended upon him,

512
00:20:33.740 --> 00:20:35.240
demanded his ID and told them

513
00:20:35.240 --> 00:20:37.510
that he was banned from American Airlines.

514
00:20:37.510 --> 00:20:40.480
And when we landed, he got escorted off the flight.

515
00:20:40.480 --> 00:20:42.960
And when when we got off the plane,

516
00:20:42.960 --> 00:20:45.310
he was sitting with a couple of cops.

517
00:20:45.310 --> 00:20:48.677
And this is a weird thing as somebody who's been

518
00:20:48.677 --> 00:20:53.130
a patron of commercial aviation for a very long time,

519
00:20:53.130 --> 00:20:55.007
I've never seen anything like that happen.

520
00:20:55.007 --> 00:20:56.870
But if you peruse social media,

521
00:20:56.870 --> 00:20:59.400
it would appear that this is happening all the time.

522
00:20:59.400 --> 00:21:03.204
It's one of those common things that you never see, right?

523
00:21:03.204 --> 00:21:04.360
Like that video,

524
00:21:04.360 --> 00:21:06.315
did you see the video of the guy

525
00:21:06.315 --> 00:21:07.975
who got duct tape to his seat

526
00:21:07.975 --> 00:21:09.749
after he was trying to fight?

527
00:21:09.749 --> 00:21:10.582
I did.
I don't know

528
00:21:10.582 --> 00:21:12.580
the circumstances around that.

529
00:21:12.580 --> 00:21:14.020
People are on edge, man.

530
00:21:14.020 --> 00:21:15.680
People are angry and

531
00:21:15.680 --> 00:21:18.090
it seems that commercial aviation

532
00:21:18.090 --> 00:21:22.520
is this Petri dish for acting out

533
00:21:22.520 --> 00:21:26.331
in a way that seems new.

534
00:21:26.331 --> 00:21:30.210
The commercial aviation is a capsule for acting out,

535
00:21:30.210 --> 00:21:31.463
it's like the new subway.

536
00:21:32.309 --> 00:21:33.142
